Account of a Vaishnav Who Relied on Omens, Disciple of Shri Gusainji

A Vaishnav traveled from Gujarat to Shri Gokul and became a disciple of Shri Gusainji. Whatever task he undertook, he always consulted omens (shakun) first.

One day, the topic of omens arose. Shri Gusainji remarked, 'Before the Lord's darshan, what use are omens?' At that moment, Shri Gusainji recited this kirtan of Surdasji:

Whichever day one meets Gopal, that alone is the auspicious day;
Astrology, rituals, and calculations are great deceivers—know them as a noose for the soul. (1)
If someone asks, I answer; unasked, such counsel is tasteless;
Lotus, fish, and frog all thirst alike when the nectar-bearing raincloud pours down. (2)
Even Bhadra is good, Bharani removes worldly bondage, and thunder or sneezing while departing matters not;
If all planets in their auspicious houses were enough, why did Sita's abduction happen? (3)
Listen, foolish bee (Uddhava), you have come to Braj only to earn endless reproach;
Says Sur, all rules, rites, and vows are not worth a cowrie shell to a true lover of the Lord. (4)

When Shri Gusainji spoke this pad from His own lotus lips, the Vaishnav made a firm resolve: 'From this day onward, I will never look at omens again.' Such was that Vaishnav's unshakable faith in Shri Gusainji's words.
